      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find $\\cos 150^\\circ$, we can use the unit circle and the fact that the cosine of an angle in the unit circle is the $x$-coordinate of the corresponding point. Since $150^\\circ$ is in the second quadrant, we will use $180^\\circ - 30^\\circ$ to find the value of $\\cos 150^\\circ$. \n\nThe cosine of $150^\\circ$ is equal to the cosine of $180^\\circ - 30^\\circ$. Using the subtracting angle identity for cosine, we know that this value is equal to $- \\cos 30^\\circ$, which is equal to $- \\frac{ \\sqrt{3}}{2}$. Thus, we have:\n\n$$\\cos 150^\\circ =  - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$$\n\nThis is the final answer, boxed \\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}.",
